  "summary": "Which multi-variable Laurent polynomials arise as the multi- variable Alexander polynomial of a link in the 3-sphere or, more generally, a ho- mology 3-sphere?",
  "detail": "Which multi-variable Laurent polynomials arise as the multi- variable Alexander polynomial of a link in the 3-sphere or, more generally, a ho- mology 3-sphere? Source: Kirby's Problems in Low-Dimensional Topology notes. Original problem number: KP-1.8. Literature notes: (1) A link in a homology sphere $Y$ has a covering corresponding to the ho- momorphism $\\pi_{1}(Y \\setminus L) \\twoheadrightarrow\\mathbb{Z}^{n}$ arising from the isomorphism $H_{1}(Y \\setminus L) \\cong$ $\\mathbb{Z}^{n}$, where $n$ is the number of components of the link $L$. The homol- ogy of this covering gives rise to a multi-variable Alexander polynomial $$ \\Delta_{L}(t_{1},\\dots,t_{n}) \\in \\mathbb{Z}[t_{1}^{\\pm 1},\\dots,t_{n}^{\\pm 1}], $$ well defined up to multiplication by units, which are all of the form $\\pm t_{1}^{\\nu_{1}}\\cdots t_{n}^{\\nu_{n}}$. (2) The problem has been solved for the single-variable Alexander polynomial of knots. If $K$ is a knot in a homology sphere, then: $$ \\bullet \\Delta_{K}(t) = \\Delta_{K}(t^{-1}) $$ $\\bullet \\Delta_{K}(1) = 1$. Moreover, any polynomial satisfying these two properties is realized by a knot in the 3-sphere (or any homology sphere) [Sei35, Lev65, Rol90]. Torres [Tor53] found analogous (necessary but not sufficient–see [Hil81, Pla86]) properties of the multi-variable Alexander polynomial of a link. It is perhaps surprising that while there is no known characterization of the Alexander polynomial of two-component links, Bailey found a charac- terization [Bai77] (discussed in [Pla86]) of their Alexander modules. A general reference for the ideas here is Hillman’s book [Hil12]. (3) The problem can be refined by focusing on specific families of links. Question (i). Which polynomials arise for a given family of knots or links? Some partial results are known. $\\bullet$ Torus links. If $T_{p,q}$ is the $(p, q)-$ torus link, let $d = \\gcd(p, q)$. Then $$ \\Delta_{Tp,q}(t_{1}, t_{2},..., t_{d}) = [(t_{1}t_{2}... t_{d})^{pq/d} - 1]^{d}[(t_{1}t_{2}... t_{d}) - 1] $$ $$ [(t_{1}t_{2}... t_{d})^{p/d} - 1][(t_{1}t_{2}... t_{d})^{q/d} - 1]. $$ See [Rol90] for knots and [EN85, Theorem 12.1]; compare [Ore21, Corollary 8.2] for links with $d > 1$. $\\bullet$ Two-bridge links. Many authors have found necessary (but still in- sufficient) conditions on the Alexander polynomial of a 2-bridge knot or link. Results of Koseleff and Pecker [KP15] subsume most earlier results. Given a 2-variable polynomial, Hoste [Hos20], gives an al- gorithm to decide if the polynomial is the Alexander polynomial of a 2-component, 2-bridge link. $\\bullet$ Alternating links. The Alexander polynomials of alternating knots have special properties [Cro59, Mur58], and their coefficients are conjectured to have various concavity properties. See Problem 1.7 for an extensive discussion of these. It would be of interest to extend such properties to the case of alternating links. (4) Similarly, any 3-manifold with $H_{1}(X) \\cong \\mathbb{Z}^{n}$ has an associated multi- variable Alexander polynomial $\\Delta_{X} \\in \\mathbb{Z}[H_{1}(X)] \\cong \\mathbb{Z}[t_{1}^{\\pm1},\\ldots,t_{n}^{\\pm1}]$. Question (ii). Which Laurent polynomials arise as the multi-variable Alexander polynomial of a 3-manifold? References cited: - [Sei35] H. Seifert. Über das Geschlecht von Knoten. Math. Ann., 110(1):571–592, 1935. doi:10.1007/BF01448044. - [Lev65] J. Levine. A characterization of knot polynomials. Topology, 4:135–141, 1965. doi: 10.1016/0040-9383(65)90061-3. - [Rol90] Dale Rolfsen. Knots and links, volume 7 of Mathematics Lecture Series. Publish or Perish, Inc., Houston, TX, 1990. Corrected reprint of the 1976 original. - [Tor53] Guillermo Torres. On the Alexander polynomial. Ann. of Math. (2), 57:57–89, 1953. doi:10.2307/1969726. - [Hil81] Jonathan A. Hillman. The Torres conditions are insufficient. Math. Proc. Cambridge Philos. Soc., 89(1):19–22, 1981. doi:10.1017/S0305004100057893. - [Pla86] M. L. Platt. Insufficiency of Torres’ conditions for two-component classical links. Trans. Amer. Math. Soc., 296(1):125–136, 1986. doi:10.2307/2000564. - [Bai77] James Leonard Bailey. ALEXANDER INVARIANTS OF LINKS. ProQuest LLC, Ann Arbor, MI, 1977. Thesis (Ph.D.)–The University of British Columbia (Canada). URL: http://gateway.proquest.com/openurl?url ver=Z39.88-2004\\& rft val fmt=info:ofi/fmt:kev:mtx:dissertation\\&res dat=xri:pqdiss\\&rft dat=xri: pqdiss:NK34756. - [Hil12] Jonathan Hillman. Algebraic invariants of links, volume 52 of Series on Knots and Everything. World Scientific Publishing Co. Pte. Ltd., Hackensack, NJ, second edition, 2012. doi:10.1142/8493. - [EN85] David Eisenbud and Walter Neumann. Three-dimensional link theory and invariants of plane curve singularities, volume 110 of Annals of Mathematics Studies. Princeton University Press, Princeton, NJ, 1985. - [Ore21] S. Yu. Orevkov. Multivariate signatures of iterated torus links. Funktsional. Anal. i Prilozhen., 55(1):73–92, 2021. English translation in Funct. Anal. Appl. 55 (2021), no. 1, 59–74. doi:10.1134/S001626632101007X. - [KP15] Pierre-Vincent Koseleff and Daniel Pecker. On Alexander-Conway polynomials of two-bridge links. J. Symbolic Comput., 68:215–229, 2015. doi:10.1016/j.jsc.2014.09.011. - [Hos20] Jim Hoste. A note on Alexander polynomials of 2-bridge links. J. Knot Theory Ramifications, 29(8):1971003, 7, 2020. doi:10.1142/S0218216519710032. - [Cro59] Richard Crowell. Genus of alternating link types. Ann. of Math. (2), 69:258–275, 1959. doi:10.2307/1970181. - [Mur58] Kunio Murasugi. On the genus of the alternating knot. I, II. J. Math. Soc. Japan, 10:94–105, 235–248, 1958. doi:10.2969/jmsj/01010094.",
