  "summary": "If Dehn surgery on a knot $K$ gives a lens space, then $K$ is a Berge knot.",
  "detail": "If Dehn surgery on a knot $K$ gives a lens space, then $K$ is a Berge knot. Source: Kirby's Problems in Low-Dimensional Topology notes. Original problem number: KP-1.9. Literature notes: (1) This appears as [Kir97, Problem 1.78]. Let $(\\Sigma; H_{1}, H_{2})$ denote the genus- two Heegaard splitting of $S^{3}$ (cf. Section 3.3). A knot $K \\subset S^{3}$ is a Berge knot if it lies on $\\Sigma$ and there are properly embedded disks $D_{i} \\subset H_{i}$ such that $|K \\cap D_{i}| = 1$. These knots are also called doubly primitive; see [Gor09, Section 3.2]. (2) The conjecture, often referred to as the Berge Conjecture, is known for small order lens spaces [Gor09]. For example, if surgery on $K$ gives $L(p, q)$ with $|p| \\leq 5$, then $K$ is the unknot or the trefoil [KMOS07]. In general, if a lens space were to arise as surgery on a non-Berge knot, that lens space must also arise as surgery on a Berge knot [Gre13b]. The putative knot necessarily has the same knot Floer homology as that corresponding Berge knot. (3) From its description, any Berge knot is necessarily a tunnel number one knot. The Berge conjecture has been verified for all tunnel number one knots in the preprint [LMP25] and all non-hyperbolic knots [Mos71, BL89]. (4) If surgery on a non-torus knot produces a lens space, then it must be an integral surgery by the Cyclic Surgery Theorem [CGLS87]. The size of the surgery coefficient is also heavily constrained. If $S^{3}_{p}(K) = L(p,q)$ for a nontrivial, non-trefoil knot $K$, then $2g(K)-1 \\leq |p|-2\\sqrt{4(|p|+1)/5}$ [Gre13b]. If $K$ is a counterexample to the Berge conjecture, then $|p| < 4g(K) - 1$ [Bak06]. (5) Using Heegaard Floer homology, it is also known that a knot with a lens space surgery must be fibered and, after possibly mirroring, strongly quasi- positive [Ni07, Hed10]. The knot Floer homology of the dual knot $\\widetilde{K}$ in $L(p,q)$ has smallest possible knot Floer homology, $$ \\dim \\widehat{\\mathrm{HFK}}(\\widetilde{K}) = \\dim \\widehat{\\mathrm{HF}}(L(p,q)) $$ [Ras07, Hed11, Gre13b]. Such a knot is called Floer simple, and one proposed strategy is to prove that Floer simple knots are one-bridge with respect to the standard genus one Heegaard splitting of $L(p, q)$ [BGH08], as Berge proved that such knots are necessarily doubly primitive [Ber18]. References cited: - [Kir97] R.C. Kirby. Problems in low–dimensional topology. In W. Kazez, editor, Geometric Topology. American Math. Soc./International Press, Providence, 1997. - [Gor09] Cameron Gordon. Dehn surgery and 3-manifolds. In Low dimensional topology, volume 15 of IAS/Park City Math. Ser., pages 21–71. Amer. Math. Soc., Providence, RI, 2009. - [KMOS07] P. Kronheimer, T. Mrowka, P. Ozsváth, and Z. Szabó. Monopoles and lens space surgeries. Ann. of Math. (2), 165(2):457–546, 2007. doi:10.4007/annals.2007.165.457. - [Gre13b] Joshua Evan Greene. The lens space realization problem. Ann. of Math. (2), 177(2):449–511, 2013. doi:10.4007/annals.2013.177.2.3. - [LMP25] Tao Li, Yoav Moriah, and Tali Pinsky. Tunnel number one knots satisfy the Berge conjecture. Geom. Topol., 29(6):3271–3343, 2025. doi:10.2140/gt.2025.29.3271. - [Mos71] Louise Moser. Elementary surgery along a torus knot. Pacific J. Math., 38:737–745, 1971. http://projecteuclid.org/euclid.pjm/1102969920. - [BL89] Steven A. Bleiler and Richard A. Litherland. Lens spaces and Dehn surgery. Proc. Amer. Math. Soc., 107(4):1127–1131, 1989. doi:10.2307/2047677. - [CGLS87] Marc Culler, C. McA. Gordon, J. Luecke, and Peter B. Shalen. Dehn surgery on knots. Ann. of Math. (2), 125(2):237–300, 1987. doi:10.2307/1971311. - [Bak06] Kenneth L. Baker. Small genus knots in lens spaces have small bridge number. Algebr. Geom. Topol., 6:1519–1621, 2006. doi:10.2140/agt.2006.6.1519. - [Ni07] Yi Ni. Knot Floer homology detects fibred knots. Invent. Math., 170(3):577–608, 2007. doi:10.1007/s00222-007-0075-9. - [Hed10] Matthew Hedden. Notions of positivity and the Ozsváth-Szabó concordance invariant. J. Knot Theory Ramifications, 19(5):617–629, 2010. doi:10.1142/S0218216510008017. - [Ras07] Jacob Rasmussen. Lens space surgeries and L-space homology spheres, 2007. arXiv: 0710.2531. - [Hed11] Matthew Hedden. On Floer homology and the Berge conjecture on knots admitting lens space surgeries. Trans. Amer. Math. Soc., 363(2):949–968, 2011. doi:10.1090/S0002-9947-2010-05117-7. - [BGH08] Kenneth L. Baker, J. Elisenda Grigsby, and Matthew Hedden. Grid diagrams for lens spaces and combinatorial knot Floer homology. Int. Math. Res. Not. IMRN, 2008(10):Art. ID rnm024, 39, 2008. doi:10.1093/imrn/rnn024. - [Ber18] John Berge. Some knots with surgeries yielding lens spaces, 2018. arXiv:1802.09722.",
  "source": "UnsolvedMath KP-1.9（kirby_low_dimensional_topology · topology）",
  "url": "https://unsolvedmath.com"
