  "summary": "(Generalized Property R Conjecture). Let $L \\subset S^{3}$ be an $n$- component link such that 0-framed Dehn surgery on $L$ results in $\\#^{n}(S^{1} \\times S^{2})$. Is there a sequ",
  "detail": "(Generalized Property R Conjecture). Let $L \\subset S^{3}$ be an $n$- component link such that 0-framed Dehn surgery on $L$ results in $\\#^{n}(S^{1} \\times S^{2})$. Is there a sequence of handleslides converting $L$ into an $n$-component unlink? Source: Kirby's Problems in Low-Dimensional Topology notes. Original problem number: KP-1.10. Literature notes: (1) An $n$-component link $L \\subset S^{3}$ whose 0-surgery is $\\#^{n}(S^{1} \\times S^{2})$ is called an R-link. An R-link is said to have generalized property R if it can be converted by handleslides to an unlink. (Note that all framings and linking numbers are zero.) Gabai proved [Gab87a] that the only 1-component R-link is the unknot, solving the Property R Conjecture. The Generalized Property R Conjecture (GPRC) posits that all R-links have generalized property R [Kir97, Problem 1.82] and was studied in [GST10]. (2) There are two ways to weaken the GPRC that are of interest. Let $L \\sqcup L'$ denote the split union of links $L$ and $L'$. Conjecture (Stable GPRC). If $L$ is an R-link, then there is an unlink $\\operatorname{U}$ such that $L \\sqcup \\operatorname{U}$ can be converted by handleslides to an unlink. Many potential counterexamples to the Stable GPRC are given in [GST10, MZ22]. (3) A Hopf pair is a canceling 1-handle/2-handle pair, which can be repre- sented in the Kirby calculus by a Hopf link with a dotted-circle component and a 0-framed component [GS99, Section 5.4]. Conjecture (Weak GPRC). If $L$ is an R-link, then there is an un- link $\\operatorname{U}$ and a split union $H$ of Hopf pairs such that $L \\sqcup \\operatorname{U} \\sqcup H$ can be converted by handleslides to the split union $\\operatorname{U}' \\sqcup H'$ of an unlink and a split union of Hopf pairs. A sufficient condition for a 2-component link to satisfy the Weak GPRC is given in [MZ22, Theorem 1.1]. (4) There are connections between these conjectures and other important problems. Given an R-link $L$, one can construct a homotopy 4-ball $B_{L}$ by attaching 0-framed 2-handles to $S^{3} \\times I$ along $L$ and capping off with a 4-dimensional 1-handlebody. Turning this handle-decomposition upside down gives a balanced presentation $P_{L}$ of the trivial group. Any homotopy 4-sphere that can be built without 1-handles is $B_{L}\\cup B^{4}$ for some R-link $L$. If an R-link L satisfies the Weak GPRC, then $B_{L}$ is diffeomorphic to $B^{4}$. So, the Weak GPRC implies the Smooth Poincaré Conjecture in dimension 4 for homotopy 4-spheres built without 1-handles. The Andrews–Curtis Conjecture (ACC–see Problem 5.10) posits that any balanced presentation of the trivial group can be converted to the trivial presentation through balanced presentations. If the GPRC is true, then so is the ACC. There is also a stable version of the ACC that is implied by the Stable GPRC [GST10, MZ22]. If $L$ is an R-link, then $L$ bounds a collection of homotopy-ribbon disks in the homotopy 4-ball $B_{L}$. Thus, such links provide potential counterex- amples to the Slice-Ribbon Conjecture (Problem 1.50) but are ribbon if they satisfy the Stable GPRC [AT16b]; see [MZ25, Proposition 5.2]. If $K$ is a fibered, homotopy-ribbon knot, then the closed monodromy of $K$ admits an extension across a handlebody [CG83a]. Any such extension gives rise to a link $L$ lying on the fiber of $K$ such that $L$ and $K \\cup L$ are both R-links, and $K \\cup L$ is ribbon if $L$ (equivalently, $K \\cup L$) satisfies the Stable GPRC. References cited: - [Gab87a] David Gabai. Foliations and the topology of 3-manifolds. II. J. Differential Geom., 26(3):461–478, 1987. http://projecteuclid.org/euclid.jdg/1214441487. - [Kir97] R.C. Kirby. Problems in low–dimensional topology. In W. Kazez, editor, Geometric Topology. American Math. Soc./International Press, Providence, 1997. - [GST10] Robert E. Gompf, Martin Scharlemann, and Abigail Thompson. Fibered knots and potential counterexamples to the property 2R and slice-ribbon conjectures. Geom. Topol., 14(4):2305–2347, 2010. doi:10.2140/gt.2010.14.2305. - [MZ22] Jeffrey Meier and Alexander Zupan. Generalized square knots and homotopy 4-spheres. J. Differential Geom., 122(1):69–129, 2022. doi:10.4310/jdg/1668186788. - [GS99] Robert E. Gompf and András I. Stipsicz. 4-manifolds and Kirby calculus, volume 20 of Graduate Studies in Mathematics. American Mathematical Society, Providence, RI, 1999. doi:10.1090/gsm/020. - [AT16b] Tetsuya Abe and Motoo Tange. A construction of slice knots via annulus twists. Michigan Math. J., 65(3):573–597, 2016. doi:10.1307/mmj/1472066149. - [MZ25] Jeffrey Meier and Alexander Zupan. Knots bounding nonisotopic ribbon disks. J. Topol., 18(4):Paper No. e70047, 18, 2025. doi:10.1112/topo.70047. - [CG83a] A. J. Casson and C. McA. Gordon. A loop theorem for duality spaces and fibred ribbon knots. Invent. Math., 74(1):119–137, 1983. doi:10.1007/BF01388533.",
  "source": "UnsolvedMath KP-1.10（kirby_low_dimensional_topology · topology）",
  "url": "https://unsolvedmath.com"
