  "summary": "(Cabling conjecture). Let $K \\subset S^{3}$ be a knot and $r \\in \\mathbb{Q}$. If $r$-framed Dehn surgery on $K$ is not prime, then $K$ is a nontrivial cable of a knot $J \\subset S^",
  "detail": "(Cabling conjecture). Let $K \\subset S^{3}$ be a knot and $r \\in \\mathbb{Q}$. If $r$-framed Dehn surgery on $K$ is not prime, then $K$ is a nontrivial cable of a knot $J \\subset S^{3}$ and $r$ is the slope of the cabling annulus. Source: Kirby's Problems in Low-Dimensional Topology notes. Original problem number: KP-1.11. Literature notes: (1) The conjecture is due to González-Acuña and Short [GAnS86]. If $K =$ $J_{p,q}$ is a cable knot, then there is an annulus in $S^{3}\\setminus\\nu(K)$ that has boundary equal to two parallel curves of slope $pq$. This annulus is called the cabling annulus and $pq$ is referred to as the slope of the cabling annulus. Using a cut and paste argument, one can show that $$ S^{3}_{pq}(K)=S^{3}_{q/p}(J)\\#S^{3}_{p/q}(\\operatorname{U}), $$ where $\\operatorname{U}$ is the unknot. Therefore, if $p \\neq 1$, then $S^{3}_{pq}(K)$ is not prime. (2) Many special cases of the conjecture are known. Gordon and Luecke [GL87] prove that if $r$ is a reducible slope, then $r$ is an integer and the surgery contains a lens space summand. The conjecture is known for satellite knots [Sch90], strongly invertible knots [EMn92], alternating knots [MT92] and symmetric knots [LZ94, HS98, HM97b]. (3) Greene [Gre15] has shown that if $r$-framed surgery on $K$ gives a connected sum of nontrivial lens spaces, then $K$ is a $(p, q)$-cable of either the unknot or a torus knot and that $r = pq$. (4) Dunfield [Dun20] observed that a similar conjecture may hold for knots in $S^{1} \\times S^{2}$. He conjectures that there are no hyperbolic knots in $S^{1} \\times S^{2}$ that have non-prime Dehn surgery. (5) A weaker version of the cabling conjecture is the two summands conjecture, which says that if $S^{3}_{r}(K)$ is non-prime, then it cannot have more than two prime summands. Note that it follows from work of Howie [How02], Sayari [Say09] and Valdez-Sánchez [VS99] that $S^{3}_{r}(K)$ can never contain more than three irreducible summands. References cited: - [GAnS86] Francisco González-Acuña and Hamish Short. Knot surgery and primeness. Math. Proc. Cambridge Philos. Soc., 99(1):89–102, 1986. doi:10.1017/S0305004100063969. - [GL87] C. McA. Gordon and J. Luecke. Only integral Dehn surgeries can yield reducible manifolds. Math. Proc. Cambridge Philos. Soc., 102(1):97–101, 1987. doi:10.1017/S0305004100067086. - [Sch90] Martin Scharlemann. Producing reducible 3-manifolds by surgery on a knot. Topology, 29(4):481–500, 1990. doi:10.1016/0040-9383(90)90017-E. - [EMn92] Mario Eudave Muñoz. Band sums of links which yield composite links. The cabling conjecture for strongly invertible knots. Trans. Amer. Math. Soc., 330(2):463–501, 1992. doi:10.2307/2153918. - [MT92] William W. Menasco and Morwen B. Thistlethwaite. Surfaces with boundary in alternating knot exteriors. J. Reine Angew. Math., 426:47–65, 1992. doi:10.1515/crll.1992.426.47. - [LZ94] E. Luft and X. Zhang. Symmetric knots and the cabling conjecture. Math. Ann., 298(3):489–496, 1994. doi:10.1007/BF01459747. - [HS98] Chuichiro Hayashi and Koya Shimokawa. Symmetric knots satisfy the cabling conjecture. Math. Proc. Cambridge Philos. Soc., 123(3):501–529, 1998. doi:10.1017/S0305004197002399. - [HM97b] Chuichiro Hayashi and Kimihiko Motegi. Dehn surgery on knots in solid tori creating essential annuli. Trans. Amer. Math. Soc., 349(12):4897–4930, 1997. doi: 10.1090/S0002-9947-97-01723-6. - [Gre15] Joshua Evan Greene. L-space surgeries, genus bounds, and the cabling conjecture. J. Differential Geom., 100(3):491–506, 2015. http://projecteuclid.org/euclid.jdg/1432842362. - [Dun20] Nathan M. Dunfield. A census of exceptional Dehn fillings. In Characters in lowdimensional topology, volume 760 of Contemp. Math., pages 143–155. Amer. Math. Soc., [Providence], RI, [2020] ©2020. doi:10.1090/conm/760/15289. - [How02] James Howie. A proof of the Scott-Wiegold conjecture on free products of cyclic groups. J. Pure Appl. Algebra, 173(2):167–176, 2002. doi:10.1016/S0022-4049(02) 00042-7. - [Say09] Nabil Sayari. Reducible Dehn surgery and the bridge number of a knot. J. Knot Theory Ramifications, 18(4):493–504, 2009. doi:10.1142/S0218216509007038. - [VS99] Luis Gerardo Valdez Sánchez. Dehn fillings of 3-manifolds and non-persistent tori. Topology Appl., 98(1-3):355–370, 1999. II Iberoamerican Conference on Topology and its Applications (Morelia, 1997). doi:10.1016/S0166-8641(99)00038-3.",
  "source": "UnsolvedMath KP-1.11（kirby_low_dimensional_topology · topology）",
  "url": "https://unsolvedmath.com"
