  "summary": "Let $K$ be a null-homotopic knot in a 3-manifold $Y$ , and let $Y_{0}(K)$ be the manifold obtained by 0-surgery on $K$. (a) Conjecture: Let $F$ be a Seifert surface for $K$, and le",
  "detail": "Let $K$ be a null-homotopic knot in a 3-manifold $Y$ , and let $Y_{0}(K)$ be the manifold obtained by 0-surgery on $K$. (a) Conjecture: Let $F$ be a Seifert surface for $K$, and let $\\widetilde{F} \\subset Y_{0}(K)$ be the closed surface obtained by capping off $\\partial F$ with a disk. If $Y_{0}(K)$ is a surface bundle over $S^{1}$ with fiber in the homology class $[\\widetilde{F}]$, then $K$ is a fibered knot with fiber in the homology class $[F]$. (b) Conjecture: If $F$ is taut in the exterior of $K$ in $Y$ , then $\\widetilde{F}$ is also taut in $Y_{0}(K)$. Source: Kirby's Problems in Low-Dimensional Topology notes. Original problem number: KP-1.13. Literature notes: (1) Conjecture (a) is an updating of [Kir97, Problem 1.80C], and is due to Boileau. Conjecture (b) is due to Gabai. (2) Let $M$ be a compact, oriented, connected 3-manifold, let $F \\subset M$ be a properly embedded connected surface. We say that $F$ is taut if it realizes the Thurston norm in the homology class $[F] \\neq 0 \\in H_{2}(M, \\partial M),$ or if $F$ is a sphere. (3) In the original version of Conjecture (a), the surgery slope can be any rational number, and there is no restriction on the homology class of the fiber. However, there are simple counterexamples to the original question [Ni09]. More precisely, there exist nonfibered null-homotopic knots such that every integral surgery on the knot yields a manifold that fibers over $S^{1}$. (4) It is known that $Y_{0}(K)$ does not contain a homologically essential 2-sphere if $Y \\setminus K$ does not contain such 2-spheres and $K$ is nontrivial [Gab87a, Lac97, HL22, Ni23b]. Conjecture (a) and Conjecture (b) are known for knots in $S^{3}$ [Gab87b], L-spaces [Ni07, AN09], and certain reducible manifolds [Gab87b, Ni13]. They are also known for knots obtained from the unknot by crossing changes of the same sign [Ni24]. References cited: - [Kir97] R.C. Kirby. Problems in low–dimensional topology. In W. Kazez, editor, Geometric Topology. American Math. Soc./International Press, Providence, 1997. - [Ni09] Yi Ni. Dehn surgeries that yield fibred 3-manifolds. Math. Ann., 344(4):863–876, 2009. doi:10.1007/s00208-008-0331-3. - [Gab87a] David Gabai. Foliations and the topology of 3-manifolds. II. J. Differential Geom., 26(3):461–478, 1987. http://projecteuclid.org/euclid.jdg/1214441487. - [Lac97] Marc Lackenby. Dehn surgery on knots in 3-manifolds. J. Amer. Math. Soc., 10(4):835–864, 1997. doi:10.1090/S0894-0347-97-00241-5. - [HL22] Jennifer Hom and Tye Lidman. Dehn surgery and nonseparating two-spheres. In Gauge theory and low-dimensional topology—progress and interaction, volume 5 of Open Book Ser., pages 145–153. Math. Sci. Publ., Berkeley, CA, 2022. https: //msp.org/obs/2022/5-1/p07.xhtml. - [Ni23b] Yi Ni. Null-homotopic knots have property R. Math. Proc. Cambridge Philos. Soc., 175(1):217–223, 2023. doi:10.1017/S0305004123000129. - [Gab87b] David Gabai. Foliations and the topology of 3-manifolds. III. J. Differential Geom., 26(3):479–536, 1987. http://projecteuclid.org/euclid.jdg/1214441488. - [Ni07] Yi Ni. Knot Floer homology detects fibred knots. Invent. Math., 170(3):577–608, 2007. doi:10.1007/s00222-007-0075-9. - [AN09] Yinghua Ai and Yi Ni. Two applications of twisted Floer homology. Int. Math. Res. Not. IMRN, 2009(19):3726–3746, 2009. doi:10.1093/imrn/rnp070. - [Ni13] Yi Ni. Nonseparating spheres and twisted Heegaard Floer homology. Algebr. Geom. Topol., 13(2):1143–1159, 2013. doi:10.2140/agt.2013.13.1143. - [Ni24] Yi Ni. Property G and the 4-genus. Trans. Amer. Math. Soc. Ser. B, 11:120–143, 2024. doi:10.1090/btran/153.",
