  "summary": "(a) Are there integral homology spheres with arbitrarily large (integral) Dehn surgery number? Are there irreducible examples? Does the connected sum of $n$ nontrivial homology sph",
  "detail": "(a) Are there integral homology spheres with arbitrarily large (integral) Dehn surgery number? Are there irreducible examples? Does the connected sum of $n$ nontrivial homology spheres have Dehn surgery number at least $n$? (b) Given a 3-manifold $Y$ with a smooth orientation-preserving $\\mathbb{Z}/n\\mathbb{Z}$ sym- metry $\\varphi$, Sakuma [Sak01] (generalizing [PS01]) proved that $Y$ admits a surgery description compatible with the symmetry. Define the equivariant (integral) Dehn surgery number of $(Y, \\varphi)$ as the minimal number of con- nected components of any such link. Do there exist $(Y, \\varphi)$ whose equivari- ant (integral) Dehn surgery number is arbitrarily larger than the (integral) Dehn surgery number? (c) Following the remarks below, we can consider the following specific case: Does there exist a manifold with a 2-fold symmetry whose equivariant (integral) Dehn surgery number is larger than the (integral) Dehn surgery number? Source: Kirby's Problems in Low-Dimensional Topology notes. Original problem number: KP-1.15. Literature notes: (1) Any oriented connected closed 3-manifold $Y$ is obtained by (integral) Dehn surgery on a link in the 3-sphere, and the minimal number of connected components of any such link is called the (integral) Dehn surgery number of $Y$ . (2) Part (a) is a version of Problem 3.102 from [Kir97], asked by Auckly. The integral Dehn surgery case is interesting to consider from the perspective of 4-manifolds bounded by $Y$ , as it is related to the minimum number of handles needed to describe the 4-manifold. There are many examples of integral homology spheres with Dehn surgery number at least 2, the earliest due to [GL87], hyperbolic examples due to [Auc97], and Seifert fibered examples due to [HKL16b]. (3) Regarding part (b), it should be possible to show that the equivari- ant surgery number does not agree with the surgery number. Consider $Y=S^{3}_{2}(4_{1})$, which is a Seifert fibered space (see, for example, [BW01, Theorem 1.1(4))]). Since $Y$ is Seifert fibered, it has a $\\mathbb{Z}/n\\mathbb{Z}$ action, for all $n$, while $4_{1}$ does not. It remains to be shown that there is no other knot $K$ for which some $2/q$-surgery is $Y$ ; a plausible approach is to use the mapping cone formula for Heegaard Floer homology. Finding the examples as in part (b) for which the equivariant surgery number is arbitrarily larger than the Dehn surgery number is, as in part (a), more challenging. (4) Lastly, we point out that this problem is closely related to Problem 3.39. References cited: - [Sak01] Makoto Sakuma. Surgery description of orientation-preserving periodic maps on compact orientable 3-manfolds. Rend. Istit. Mat. Univ. Trieste, 32:375–396, 2001. Dedicated to the memory of Marco Reni. - [PS01] Józef H. Przytycki and Maxim V. Sokolov. Surgeries on periodic links and homology of periodic 3-manifolds. Math. Proc. Cambridge Philos. Soc., 131(2):295–307, 2001. doi:10.1017/S0305004101005308. - [Kir97] R.C. Kirby. Problems in low–dimensional topology. In W. Kazez, editor, Geometric Topology. American Math. Soc./International Press, Providence, 1997. - [GL87] C. McA. Gordon and J. Luecke. Only integral Dehn surgeries can yield reducible manifolds. Math. Proc. Cambridge Philos. Soc., 102(1):97–101, 1987. doi:10.1017/S0305004100067086. - [Auc97] David Auckly. Surgery numbers of 3-manifolds: a hyperbolic example. In Geometric topology (Athens, GA, 1993), volume 2 of AMS/IP Stud. Adv. Math., pages 21–34. Amer. Math. Soc., Providence, RI, 1997. doi:10.1090/amsip/002.1/02. - [HKL16b] Jennifer Hom, Çağrı Karakurt, and Tye Lidman. Surgery obstructions and Heegaard Floer homology. Geom. Topol., 20(4):2219–2251, 2016. doi:10.2140/gt.2016.20.2219. - [BW01] Mark Brittenham and Ying-Qing Wu. The classification of exceptional Dehn surgeries on 2-bridge knots. Comm. Anal. Geom., 9(1):97–113, 2001. doi:10.4310/CAG.2001.v9.n1.a4.",
