  "summary": "(a) Given a knot $K \\subset S^{3}$ determine all knots $K' \\subset S^{3}$ for which the branched double covers of $S^{3}$ along $K$ and $K'$ are homeomorphic. (b) Find a set of mov",
  "detail": "(a) Given a knot $K \\subset S^{3}$ determine all knots $K' \\subset S^{3}$ for which the branched double covers of $S^{3}$ along $K$ and $K'$ are homeomorphic. (b) Find a set of moves on links in $S^{3}$ so that two links are related by these moves if and only if they have the same branched double cover. Source: Kirby's Problems in Low-Dimensional Topology notes. Original problem number: KP-1.16. Literature notes: (1) These are Problems 3.25 and 1.22 in [Kir97], respectively. See Prob- lem 1.17 for a related problem about alternating links. Let $\\Sigma(K)$ denote the branched double cover of $S^{3}$ along $K$. (2) It follows from the Orbifold Geometrization Theorem that there are finitely many $K'$ with $\\Sigma(K') \\cong \\Sigma(K)$. See [Pao05] for a discussion. (3) Mecchia and Zimmerman showed that if $\\Sigma(K)$ is hyperbolic, then there are at most eight other $K'$ with $\\Sigma(K') \\cong \\Sigma(K)$ [MZ04]. Kawauchi showed that this bound is sharp, exhibiting infinitely many 9-tuples of distinct hy- perbolic knots in $S^{3}$ with homeomorphic branched double covers [Kaw06]. However, his construction is very non-explicit, and it would be interest- ing to have some explicit constructions. Mecchia further described how hyperbolic knots with the same double branched cover can be understood in terms of isometries of the cover [Mec01] (4) If $K$ is a 2-bridge knot and $\\Sigma(K') \\cong \\Sigma(K)$ then $K' = K [\\mathrm{HR}85$]. (5) If $K$ and $K'$ are related by Conway mutation then $\\Sigma(K') \\cong \\Sigma(K)$. There are also non-mutant knots with the same branched double covers, includ- ing $T(3, 7)$ and $P(-2, 3, 7).$ In this example, the Khovanov homologies have the same total rank, but Watson [Wat10] gives non-mutant knots with homeomorphic branched double covers the total rank of whose Kho- vanov homologies differ, showing that the rank of Khovanov homology is not an invariant of branched double covers. See also [Pao01]. (6) Higher order cyclic branched covers are generally much better knot in- variants. For example, work of Zimmerman [Zim98] shows that if $n \\geq 3$ and $K \\subset S^{3}$ is hyperbolic, then the $n$-fold cyclic branched cover of $K$ distinguishes $K$ from other knots so long as $K$ does not admit peri- odic symmetries of order $n$, and the latter condition is satisfied whenever $n > 2g(K) + 1$. See [Pao05] for further discussion. (7) Extending work of Montesinos [Mon85], Piergallini constructed a com- plete set of moves relating links with the same simple, irregular 3-fold branched covers [Pie91]. References cited: - [Kir97] R.C. Kirby. Problems in low–dimensional topology. In W. Kazez, editor, Geometric Topology. American Math. Soc./International Press, Providence, 1997. - [Pao05] Luisa Paoluzzi. Hyperbolic knots and cyclic branched covers. Publ. Mat., 49(2):257– 284, 2005. doi:10.5565/PUBLMAT\\\\_49205\\\\_01. - [MZ04] Mattia Mecchia and Bruno Zimmermann. The number of knots and links with the same 2-fold branched covering. Q. J. Math., 55(1):69–76, 2004. doi:10.1093/qjmath/55.1.69. - [Kaw06] Akio Kawauchi. Topological imitations and Reni-Mecchia-Zimmermann’s conjecture. Kyungpook Math. J., 46(1):1–9, 2006. - [Mec01] Mattia Mecchia. Hyperbolic 2-fold branched coverings. Rend. Istit. Mat. Univ. Trieste, 32:165–180, 2001. Dedicated to the memory of Marco Reni. - [HR85] Craig Hodgson and J. H. Rubinstein. Involutions and isotopies of lens spaces. In Knot theory and manifolds (Vancouver, B.C., 1983), volume 1144 of Lecture Notes in Math., pages 60–96. Springer, Berlin, 1985. doi:10.1007/BFb0075012. - [Wat10] Liam Watson. A remark on Khovanov homology and two-fold branched covers. Pacific J. Math., 245(2):373–380, 2010. doi:10.2140/pjm.2010.245.373. - [Pao01] Luisa Paoluzzi. On hyperbolic type involutions. Rend. Istit. Mat. Univ. Trieste, 32:221–256, 2001. Dedicated to the memory of Marco Reni. - [Zim98] Bruno Zimmermann. On hyperbolic knots with homeomorphic cyclic branched coverings. Math. Ann., 311(4):665–673, 1998. doi:10.1007/s002080050205. - [Mon85] José Marı́a Montesinos. Lectures on 3-fold simple coverings and 3-manifolds. In Combinatorial methods in topology and algebraic geometry (Rochester, N.Y., 1982), volume 44 of Contemp. Math., pages 157–177. Amer. Math. Soc., Providence, RI, 1985. doi:10.1090/conm/044/813111. - [Pie91] R. Piergallini. Covering moves. Trans. Amer. Math. Soc., 325(2):903–920, 1991. doi:10.2307/2001654.",
  "source": "UnsolvedMath KP-1.16（kirby_low_dimensional_topology · topology）",
  "url": "https://unsolvedmath.com"
