  "id": "um-top-2691",
  "latex": "(a) Recover the Jones polynomial of links $L \\subset \\mathbb{R}^{3}$ …",
  "name": "Kirby Problem 1.32",
  "en": "Kirby Problem 1.32",
  "importance": 0.77,
  "status": "open",
  "field": "topo",
  "year": null,
  "proposer": "",
  "summary": "(a) Recover the Jones polynomial of links $L \\subset \\mathbb{R}^{3}$ by counting solutions to the Kapustin–Witten equations on $\\mathbb{R}^{3} \\times \\mathbb{R}_{+}$ with Nahm pole",
  "detail": "(a) Recover the Jones polynomial of links $L \\subset \\mathbb{R}^{3}$ by counting solutions to the Kapustin–Witten equations on $\\mathbb{R}^{3} \\times \\mathbb{R}_{+}$ with Nahm pole boundary conditions; (b) Recover the Khovanov homology of links $L \\subset \\mathbb{R}^{3}$ by counting solutions to the Haydys–Witten equations on $\\mathbb{R} \\times \\mathbb{R}^{3} \\times \\mathbb{R}_{+}$ with Nahm pole boundary conditions; (c) Use the Kapustin–Witten or Haydys–Witten equations to construct invari- ants of links in other 3-manifolds. Source: Kirby's Problems in Low-Dimensional Topology notes. Original problem number: KP-1.32. Literature notes: (1) Khovanov homology [Kho00] is a link invariant defined combinatorially, whose Euler characteristic is the Jones polynomial. It has many properties similar to Floer homologies (e.g., functoriality under cobordisms) and, as such, it is natural to ask if it has an interpretation in terms of gauge theory. Such an interpretation was proposed by Witten in [Wit12], and is summarized in points (a) and (b) of the problem. (2) The Kapustin-Witten (KW) equations are a set of gauge invariant PDE’s in four dimensions introduced in [KW07]. The Nahm pole boundary conditions for these equations are described in [Wit12]; they have a more special form along the link $L \\times \\{0\\} \\subset \\mathbb{R}^{3} \\times \\{0\\}.$ Gaiotto and Witten [GW12] sketched an approach to showing that the count of solutions to the KW equations with Nahm pole boundary conditions gives the coeffi- cients of the Jones polynomial. However, there is still work to be done to make this mathematically rigorous. (3) The Haydys-Witten (HW) equations were introduced in [Wit12] and [Hay15]. Witten conjectured that, if one forms a complex whose gen- erators are the KW solutions and whose differentials count HW solutions (with appropriate limits and boundary conditions), then its homology is Khovanov homology. (4) Part of the appeal of these gauge-theoretic ideas is that they may pro- vide a definition of the Jones polynomial/Khovanov homology manifestly independent of the link diagram. Moreover, we can write the same equa- tions with $\\mathbb{R}^{3}$ replaced by another 3-manifold, leading to part (c) of the problem. Optimistically, the counts of KW solutions could give the class of the link $L$ in the Kauffman bracket skein module of the 3-manifold [Prz91, Tur88], or be related to the $\\widetilde{Z}$ invariants predicted by physi- cists [GPV17, GPPV20]; see Problem 3.68. More ambitiously, the count of HW solutions should give analogues of Khovanov homology for links in 3-manifolds. At the moment, Khovanov homology is only de- fined in a combinatorial, concrete way for links in $S^{3}$, interval bundles over surfaces [APS04], $\\mathbb{R}$ P $^{3}$ [Man07, Gab13], and connected sums of $S^{1} \\times S^{2}$ [Roz10, Wil21]. If indeed the Haydys-Kapustin-Witten pro- gram can be carried through, it would also be important to compare the result with the Khovanov lasagna skein modules of Morrison-Walker- Wedrich [MWW22]. (5) Mathematicians have made progress on understanding the analytic prop- erties of the KW and HW equations; see [MW14], [MW20], [Tau13], [LT20], [He19], [HM19a], [Tau18], [Tau19]. Some of the compact- ness results (e.g. [Tau18, Theorem B]) only hold when the 3-manifold has positive Ricci curvature, which suggests that part (c) should first be attempted under this assumption. References cited: - [Kho00] Mikhail Khovanov. A categorification of the Jones polynomial. Duke Math. J., 101(3):359–426, 2000. doi:10.1215/S0012-7094-00-10131-7. - [Wit12] Edward Witten. Fivebranes and knots. Quantum Topol., 3(1):1–137, 2012. doi: 10.4171/QT/26. - [KW07] Anton Kapustin and Edward Witten. Electric-magnetic duality and the geometric Langlands program. Commun. Number Theory Phys., 1(1):1–236, 2007. doi:10.4310/CNTP.2007.v1.n1.a1. - [GW12] Davide Gaiotto and Edward Witten. Knot invariants from four-dimensional gauge theory. Adv. Theor. Math. Phys., 16(3):935–1086, 2012. doi:10.4310/atmp.2012.v16.n3.a5. - [Hay15] Andriy Haydys. Fukaya-Seidel category and gauge theory. J. Symplectic Geom., 13(1):151–207, 2015. doi:10.4310/JSG.2015.v13.n1.a5. - [Prz91] Józef H. Przytycki. Skein modules of 3-manifolds. Bull. Polish Acad. Sci. Math., 39(1-2):91–100, 1991. - [Tur88] V. G. Turaev. The Conway and Kauffman modules of a solid torus. Zap. Nauchn. Sem. Leningrad. Otdel. Mat. Inst. Steklov. (LOMI), 167(Issled. Topol. 6):79–89, 190, 1988. doi:10.1007/BF01099241. - [GPV17] Sergei Gukov, Pavel Putrov, and Cumrun Vafa. Fivebranes and 3-manifold homology. J. High Energy Phys., 2017(7):071, front matter+80, 2017. doi:10.1007/JHEP07(2017)071. - [GPPV20] Sergei Gukov, Du Pei, Pavel Putrov, and Cumrun Vafa. BPS spectra and 3-manifold invariants. J. Knot Theory Ramifications, 29(2):2040003, 85, 2020. doi:10.1142/S0218216520400039. - [APS04] Marta M. Asaeda, Józef H. Przytycki, and Adam S. Sikora. Categorification of the Kauffman bracket skein module of I-bundles over surfaces. Algebr. Geom. Topol., 4:1177–1210, 2004. doi:10.2140/agt.2004.4.1177. - [Man07] Vassily Olegovich Manturov. Khovanov homology for virtual knots with arbitrary coefficients. J. Knot Theory Ramifications, 16(3):345–377, 2007. doi:10.1142/S0218216507005336. - [Gab13] Boštjan Gabrovšek. The categorification of the Kauffman bracket Skein module of $\\mathbb{RP}^{3}$. Bull. Aust. Math. Soc., 88(3):407–422, 2013. doi:10.1017/S0004972713000105. - [Roz10] Lev Rozansky. A categorification of the stable SU(2) Witten-Reshetikhin-Turaev invariant of links in $S^{2}$ $\\times$ $S^{1}$, 2010. arXiv:1011.1958. - [Wil21] Michael Willis. Khovanov homology for links in \\#rp$S^{2}$ $\\times$ $S^{1}$q. Michigan Math. J., 70(4):675–748, 2021. doi:10.1307/mmj/1594281620. - [MWW22] Scott Morrison, Kevin Walker, and Paul Wedrich. Invariants of 4-manifolds from Khovanov-Rozansky link homology. Geom. Topol., 26(8):3367–3420, 2022. doi:10.2140/gt.2022.26.3367. - [MW14] Rafe Mazzeo and Edward Witten. The Nahm pole boundary condition. In The influence of Solomon Lefschetz in geometry and topology, volume 621 of Contemp. Math., pages 171–226. Amer. Math. Soc., Providence, RI, 2014. doi:10.1090/conm/621/12422. - [MW20] Rafe Mazzeo and Edward Witten. The KW equations and the Nahm pole boundary condition with knots. Comm. Anal. Geom., 28(4):871–942, 2020. doi:10.4310/CAG.2020.v28.n4.a4. - [Tau13] Clifford Henry Taubes. Compactness theorems for SL(2;C) generalizations of the 4-dimensional anti-self dual equations, 2013. arXiv:1307.6447. - [LT20] Naichung Conan Leung and Ryosuke Takahashi. Energy bound for Kapustin-Witten solutions on $S^{3}$ $\\times$ R+. Int. Math. Res. Not. IMRN, 2020(19):6135–6148, 2020. doi: 10.1093/imrn/rny198. - [He19] Siqi He. A gluing theorem for the Kapustin-Witten equations with a Nahm pole. J. Topol., 12(3):855–915, 2019. doi:10.1112/topo.12102. - [HM19a] Siqi He and Rafe Mazzeo. The extended Bogomolny equations and generalized Nahm pole boundary condition. Geom. Topol., 23(5):2475–2517, 2019. doi:10.2140/gt.2019.23.2475. - [Tau18] Clifford Henry Taubes. Sequences of Nahm pole solutions to the SU(2) KapustinWitten equations, 2018. arXiv:1805.02773. - [Tau19] Clifford Henry Taubes. The R invariant solutions to the Kapustin Witten equations on $(0,8)$ $\\times$ $\\mathbb{R}^{2}$ $\\times$ R with generalized Nahm pole asymptotics, 2019. arXiv:1903.03539.",
  "source": "UnsolvedMath KP-1.32（kirby_low_dimensional_topology · topology）",
  "url": "https://unsolvedmath.com"
