  "summary": "If a finite system of left cosets of subgroups of a group $G$ partitions $G$, then must at least two of the subgroups have the same index in $G$?",
  "detail": "If a finite system of left cosets of subgroups of a group $G$ partitions $G$, then must at least two of the subgroups have the same index in $G$? The Herzog-Schönheim conjecture, proposed in 1974, concerns coset decompositions of groups. If $G$ is partitioned by cosets $g_1H_1, g_2H_2, \\ldots, g_kH_k$ where each $H_i$ is a subgroup of $G$ and the cosets are pairwise disjoint, the conjecture states that at least two of the indices $[G:H_i]$ must be equal. While proven for abelian groups and various other special cases, the general conjecture remains open. It has connections to combinatorial number theory and the structure theory of groups.",
