  "detail": "Let $d \\geq 3$ be an odd integer. Give bounds on $\\nu(d)$ such that if $n > \\nu(d)$ the following is true: given any homogeneous polynomial $F(\\mathbf{x}) \\in \\mathbb{Z}[x_1, \\dots, x_n]$ of degree $d$, there is some $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{Z}^n \\setminus \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}$ such that $F(\\mathbf{x}) = 0$. This asks for explicit bounds on how many variables are needed to guarantee integer zeros of homogeneous polynomials. Classical results give existence but quantitative bounds remain challenging, especially for higher degrees.",
