  "proposer": "Péter Frankl",
  "summary": "For every finite union-closed family of sets (other than the empty family), there exists an element that belongs to at least half of the sets.",
  "detail": "For every finite union-closed family of sets (other than the empty family), there exists an element that belongs to at least half of the sets. Proposed by Péter Frankl in 1979, this is one of the best-known open problems in combinatorics. A union-closed family is a collection of sets closed under taking unions. Despite its simple statement, the conjecture has attracted many attempted proofs. Recent progress (2022-2024) has shown lower bounds: some element must be in at least 1% of sets (Gilmer 2022), improved to 38% of sets (2024).",
