  "summary": "Suppose that $A \\subset [N]$ has no more than $\\varepsilon N^2$ solutions to $x + y = z$. Can one remove $\\varepsilon' N$ elements to leave a sum-free set, where $\\varepsilon' \\to ",
  "detail": "Suppose that $A \\subset [N]$ has no more than $\\varepsilon N^2$ solutions to $x + y = z$. Can one remove $\\varepsilon' N$ elements to leave a sum-free set, where $\\varepsilon' \\to 0$ as $\\varepsilon \\to 0$, with a reasonable bound? It is known that one can remove $\\varepsilon' N$ elements to obtain a sum-free set, but the quantitative dependence of $\\varepsilon'$ on $\\varepsilon$ is very poor. Finding explicit reasonable bounds would significantly improve our understanding of the structure of almost sum-free sets.",
