  "detail": "Let $n$ be a positive integer. For a finite group $K$ and an automorphism $\\varphi$ of $K$ of order dividing $n$, let $$ X_{n,\\varphi}(K):=\\{x\\in K\\mid x x^{\\varphi}\\cdots x^{\\varphi^{n-1}}=1\\}. $$ Let $c_n$ be the supremum of the ratios $|X_{n,\\varphi}(H)|/|H|$ over all finite groups $H$ and their automorphisms $\\varphi\\in\\operatorname{Aut}(H)$ such that $\\varphi^n=\\operatorname{id}$ and $X_{n,\\varphi}(H)\\ne H$. (a) Let $n>1$ be a positive integer such that $c_d<1$ for all prime power divisors $d$ of $n$. Is it true that $c_n<1$? (b) For a finite group $G$ and a positive integer $n$, the generalized Hughes--Thompson subgroup is defined as $H_n(G)=\\langle x\\in G\\mid x^n\\ne 1\\rangle$. Suppose that $n$ is a positive integer for which there is a positive integer $k_n$ depending only on $n$ such that $|G:H_n(G)|\\leqslant k_n$ for all finite groups $G$ with $H_n(G)\\ne 1$. Is it true that then $c_n<1$? Source: Kourovka Notebook, New Problems, 21st issue, 2026. Original problem number: 21.1. Discussion and literature: This question is open even when $n\\geqslant 5$ is prime. A. Abdollahi, M. S. Malekan",
