  "detail": "Let $p$ be a prime. Let $G$ be a transitive subgroup of the group of finitary permutations $\\operatorname{FSym}(\\Omega)$ of a set $\\Omega$, let $N$ be a normal subgroup of $G$, and let $S$ be a transitive Sylow $p$-subgroup of $G$. (a) Is it true that $S\\cap N$ is a Sylow $p$-subgroup of $N$? (b) Is it true that $SN/N$ is a Sylow $p$-subgroup of $G/N$? (c) Are any two transitive Sylow $p$-subgroups of $G$ locally conjugate in $G$? Two subgroups $X,Y$ of a group $G$ are said to be locally conjugate if there is a locally inner automorphism $\\phi$ of $G$ such that $X^\\phi=Y$. An automorphism $\\phi$ of $G$ is said to be locally inner if for every finite subset $A\\subseteq G$ there is an element $g=g(A)\\in G$ such that $a^\\phi=g^{-1}ag$ for all $a\\in A$. Source: Kourovka Notebook, New Problems, 21st issue, 2026. Original problem number: 21.5. Discussion and literature: A. O. Asar",
