  "summary": "Problem Does every bounded linear operator on an infinite-dimensional separable Hilbert space have a non-trivial closed invariant subspace?",
  "detail": "Problem Does every bounded linear operator on an infinite-dimensional separable Hilbert space have a non-trivial closed invariant subspace? Source: Open Problem Garden. Original node ID: 36697. URL: http://www.openproblemgarden.org/op/invariant_subspace_problem. Source subject path: Analysis. Source importance: High ✭✭✭. Discussion and literature: Source-derived literature/context: - Source: http://www.openproblemgarden.org/op/invariant_subspace_problem - Subject(s): Analysis - Keywords: subspace - Importance: High ✭✭✭ - Recommended for undergraduates: no - Posted: February 9th, 2009 by tchow Problem-page discussion: Let $H$ be a Hilbert space. The subspaces $\\{0\\}$ and $H$ are trivially invariant under any linear operator on $H$, and so these are referred to as the trivial invariant subspaces. The problem is concerned with determining whether bounded operators necessarily have non-trivial invariant subspaces. This is one of the most famous open problems in functional analysis. Enflo [1] constructed Banach spaces for which the corresponding question has a negative answer, and recently Argyros and Haydon constructed a Banach space for which the corresponding question has a positive answer [4]. For a nice overview to the problem see [2], [3] or [5]. Bibliography: [1] P. Enflo, On the invariant subspace problem for Banach spaces, Acta Math. 158 (1987), 213-313. MathSciNet [2] B. S. Yadav, The Present State and Heritages of the Invariant Subspace Problem, Milan J. Math. 73 (2005), 289-316. MathSciNet another link [3] H. Radjavi and P. Rosenthal, The Invariant Subspace Problem, The Mathematics Intelligencer 4 (1982), no. 1, 33-37. MathSciNet [4] S. A. Argyros and R. G. Haydon, A hereditarily indecomposable $L_\\infty$-space that solves the scalar-plus-compact problem, arXiv:0903.3921 (2009). [5] J. Noel. The Invariant Subspace Problem. Honours Thesis, Thompson Rivers University. Link to pdf. Bibliography links: - MathSciNet: http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=0892591 - MathSciNet: http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=2175046 - another link: http://www.math.leidenuniv.nl/%7Enaw/serie5/deel06/jun2005/pdf/yadav.pdf - H. Radjavi: http://www.math.uwaterloo.ca/PM_Dept/Homepages/Radjavi/radjavi.shtml - P. Rosenthal: http://www.math.toronto.edu/rosent/ - MathSciNet: http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=0678734 - arXiv:0903.3921: http://www.arxiv.org/abs/0903.3921 - J. Noel: http://www.math.mcgill.ca/jnoel - Link to pdf: http://www.math.mcgill.ca/jnoel/pdf/Honours.pdf Comments: - April 11th, 2011 | Anonymous | closed: Does every bounded linear operator on an infinite-dimensional separable Hilbert space have a non-trivial closed invariant subspace? - April 1st, 2011 | Anonymous | ستار اكاديمي 8: ستار اكاديمي 8 thank you man, u'r good:) Computation notes: Finite compute signal: - No local finite computation has been run for this source-derived note. - This record was generated from the Open Problem Garden page text, discussion, bibliography, and comments. - LaTeX expressions extracted from the source text: 3. Attempt notes: Target: Make progress on \"Invariant subspace problem\" in Analysis, starting from the source statement and discussion. Step A: Normalize definitions Extract the precise objects, parameters, quantifiers, and known examples from the problem statement. Step B: Literature alignment Use the discussion, bibliography, and comments above to identify known bounds, reductions, solved special cases, or claimed resolutions. Step C: Attack route selection Choose between proof search, counterexample construction, finite computation, or literature verification depending on the problem type. Concrete Blocking Lemma (Most Critical): A problem-specific lemma is still needed; this note has not supplied a new proof beyond the source-derived context.",
