  "summary": "Question Give a necessary and sufficient criterion for the sequence $(a_n)$ so that the power series $\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty} a_n x^n$ is bounded for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.",
  "detail": "Question Give a necessary and sufficient criterion for the sequence $(a_n)$ so that the power series $\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty} a_n x^n$ is bounded for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Source: Open Problem Garden. Original node ID: 36928. URL: http://www.openproblemgarden.org/op/criterion_for_boundedness_of_power_series. Source subject path: Analysis. Source importance: Low ✭. Discussion and literature: Source-derived literature/context: - Source: http://www.openproblemgarden.org/op/criterion_for_boundedness_of_power_series - Author(s): Rüdinger, Andreas - Subject(s): Analysis - Keywords: boundedness; power series; real analysis - Importance: Low ✭ - Recommended for undergraduates: yes - Posted: May 9th, 2009 by andreasruedinger Problem-page discussion: Consider a power series $\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty} a_n x^n$ that is convergent for all $x \\in {\\mathbb R}$, thus defining a function $f: {\\mathbb R} \\to {\\mathbb R}$. Are there criteria to decide whether $f$ is bounded (which e.g. is the case for the series with $a_n = (-1)^k/(2k)!$ for $n = 2k$ and $a_n = 0$ for n odd)? Some general remarks: - A necessary condition for $\\sum_n a_n x^n$ to be bounded is that $a_0$ is the only non-zero $a_n$ or there are infinitely many non-zero $a_n$ 's which change sign infinitely many times. - Changing a finite set of $a_n$ 's (except $a_0$ ) does leave the subspace of bounded power series. - The subspace of bounded power series is \"large\" in the sense that it is both a linear subspace (closed under sums and scalar multiples) and an algebra (closed under products). It includes all functions of the form $a \\cos( f(x))$, where $f$ is any entire function $\\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$. The question whether the subspace of bounded power series contains only these functions seems to be open. Comments: - February 10th, 2011 | Anonymous | A necessary condition: It seems the sum would be bounded if there are only finitely many non-zero a sub n; it is not apparent to me that a sub 0 be the only non-zero a sub n. - June 21st, 2012 | Anonymous | What you have then is a: What you have then is a polynomial, and any nonconstant polynomial function is unbounded. - February 16th, 2011 | Comet | Re: A necessary condition: I posted the above comment anonymously, but now I have created an account. \"It seems the sum would be bounded if there are only finitely many non-zero a sub n; it is not apparent to me that a sub 0 be the only non-zero a sub n.\" - April 27th, 2012 | Anonymous | harder than that: Look at sin(x)=x-x^3/6+x^5/120-.... JPB - June 21st, 2012 | Anonymous | sin x = cos(pi/2 - x): The sine function is in the class mentioned. Computation notes: Finite compute signal: - No local finite computation has been run for this source-derived note. - This record was generated from the Open Problem Garden page text, discussion, bibliography, and comments. - LaTeX expressions extracted from the source text: 14. Attempt notes: Target: Make progress on \"Criterion for boundedness of power series\" in Analysis, starting from the source statement and discussion. Step A: Normalize definitions Extract the precise objects, parameters, quantifiers, and known examples from the problem statement. Step B: Literature alignment Use the discussion, bibliography, and comments above to identify known bounds, reductions, solved special cases, or claimed resolutions. Step C: Attack route selection Choose between proof search, counterexample construction, finite computation, or literature verification depending on the problem type. Concrete Blocking Lemma (Most Critical): A problem-specific lemma is still needed; this note has not supplied a new proof beyond the source-derived context.",
