  "detail": "Given the width of a tic-tac-toe board, what is the smallest dimension guaranteeing X has a winning strategy? Classic tic-tac-toe is a draw with perfect play. In higher dimensions (n^d game), questions become more complex. The Hales-Jewett theorem guarantees that for any fixed line length n, there exists a dimension d where the first player wins. But finding the exact threshold dimension for each n remains open. This connects combinatorics, game theory, and Ramsey theory.",
