  "field": "algebra",
  "year": null,
  "proposer": "",
  "summary": "Does every countable graph admit a partition where every vertex has at least as many neighbors outside its part as inside?",
  "detail": "Does every countable graph admit a partition where every vertex has at least as many neighbors outside its part as inside? The unfriendly partition conjecture asks if vertices can be partitioned into two sets such that each vertex has at least as many \"unfriendly\" neighbors (in the other set) as \"friendly\" ones (in its own set). Proven for finite graphs, but open for countably infinite graphs. This problem combines graph theory with infinite combinatorics and has connections to social network models.",
